<p>The five-member international jury of the 61st Venice Biennale art exhibition resigned April 30, days before the event is due to open May 9 in Venice, organizers said. The dispute followed the Biennale Foundation’s decision to allow Russia to participate again; Russia’s pavilion had been closed since Moscow launched its full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022. A week before resigning, the jury said it would exclude from prizes countries whose leaders are accused by the International Criminal Court of crimes against humanity or war crimes, a move described as affecting Russia and Israel. Afterward, the Biennale said it would change the awards process and let visitors vote for the top prizes, with awards to be presented on the final day of the exhibition rather than at the opening.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/a--I2qyf5-4BB6W-p33KmLyYwxBQNVs6TZfuTg5o9l504FeJV8dVFJcwNhwPXV5vbWXEcBsMH9x10mvknTJn0p6QFdomW3MNvgrWTugN1ghKYjM_Z3BfGpZYvmaKsf-9SWdetQrd9j1oOED8w94vvF0W-1MdEV2qmAn86CXrZyQGpZFzutgQh18g0obgo3q8adFEycPIzmlERYwbLz3nM67WdFCDDkZT1r_74A7sKGx7h7gxo1zWug1GQBrWNcBsF7jA8w' alt='The Russian pavilion at the Venice Biennale.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Jury members: Brazilian curator Solange Farkas led the departing panel, which also included Zoe Butt, Elvira Dyangani Ose, Marta Kuzma and Giovanna Zapperi.</li><li>Resignation notice: The jurors issued a brief signed statement through e-flux, The National reported.</li><li>Prize categories: The revised vote covers the Golden Lion for best participant and the Golden Lion for best national participation.</li><li>Opening boycott: Italian Culture Minister Alessandro Giuli had said he would boycott the Biennale’s opening amid the pavilion dispute.</li></ul><blockquote>The message is clear: there is no place for politics, boycotts, or antisemitism in the cultural world - Gideon Sa&#x27;ar</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Venice Biennale Foundation: The foundation said the revised awards process would treat all national participants equally and said exclusion or censorship was inconsistent with the Biennale’s founding spirit. (<a href='https://www.agi.it/cronaca/news/2026-04-30/biennale-venezia-dimessa-giuria-internazionale-36838572/'>AGI</a>)</li><li>International jury: In the statement announcing its awards policy, the panel said it wanted to reaffirm its commitment to defending human rights. (<a href='https://kurier.at/kultur/kunst-messen-biennale-venedig-jury-ruecktritt/403155682'>Kurier</a>)</li><li>Italian government: Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni said the government disagreed with the foundation’s decision on the Russian pavilion but recognized the Biennale as an autonomous body. (<a href='https://tg24.sky.it/cronaca/2026/04/30/biennale-venezia-dimissioni-giuria-internazionale'>Sky TG24</a>)</li><li>European Commission: EU Culture Commissioner Glenn Micallef praised Giuli’s stance on the Russian pavilion, calling it a principled position. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/venice-biennale-jury-quits-over-decision-allow-russia-participation/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Israel: The Foreign Ministry objected to applying the awards ban to participating artist Belu-Simion Fainaru, calling it a contamination of the art world. (<a href='https://kurier.at/kultur/kunst-messen-biennale-venedig-jury-ruecktritt/403155682'>Kurier</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/venice-biennale-jury-quits-over-decision-allow-russia-participation/'>Venice Biennale jury quits over decision to allow Russia’s participation</a> - politico.eu</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/05/01/g-s1-119661/the-venice-biennale-jury-resigns'>The Venice Biennale jury resigns amid tensions over awards ban, Russian participation</a> - npr.org</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/2026/apr/30/venice-biennale-jury-quits-amid-row-russia'>Venice Biennale jury quits amid row over participation of Russia</a> - theguardian.com</li><li